 aged men and women in relation to lifetime socioeconomic status: evidence from a national birth cohort</article-title><source>J Epidemiol Community Health</source><year>2003</year><volume>57</volume><fpage>816</fpage><lpage>22</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14573589</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Muth&#x000e9;n</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Muth&#x000e9;n</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><source>Mplus: Statistical Analysis with Latent Variables.</source><year>1998</year><publisher-loc>Los Angeles, CA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Muth&#x000e9;n and Muth&#x000e9;n</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Richards</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Sacker</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Lifetime antecedents of cognitive reserve</article-title><source>J Clin Exp Neuropsychol</source><year>2003</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>614</fpage><lpage>24</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12815499</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Singh-Manoux</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Richards</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Marmot</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Socioeconomic position across the lifecourse: how does it relate to cognitive function in mid-life?</article-title><source>Ann Epidemiol</source><year>2005</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>572</fpage><lpage>78</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16118001</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>De Stavola</surname><given-names>BL</given-names></name><name><surname>Nitsch</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Dos</surname><given-names>SSI</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Statistical issues in life course epidemiology</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>2006</year><volume>163</volume><fpage>84</fpage><lpage>96</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16306313</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kaufman</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name><name><surname>Cooper</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Seeking causal explanations in social epidemiology</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>1999</year><volume>150</volume><fpage>113</fpage><lpage>20</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10412955</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kaufman</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Specifying counterfactual contrasts for the health effects of socioeconomic position at different points in the life-course</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>2008</year><volume>167</volume><fpage>S137</fpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>